course: dictionary
lesson: 12645
title: Displacement: moving things out of place
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: «Displacement» is a noun. It comes from «displace», which means to move something out of its usual place or position. The same word is used in science and in the news, so the context clues tell you which meaning is needed.
explain: «The heavy box caused a displacement of the books on the shelf.» Here the box pushed the books out of their place. In science, «displacement» means the water pushed aside when an object goes into it. «When Mia put the stone in the jug, the water level rose.» The water moved aside to make room for the stone.
explain: In the news and in geography, «displacement of people» means forcing people to leave their homes. «The floods caused the displacement of thousands of families.» The clue words floods and families tell you that people had to leave home. Nothing moved on a shelf here.
fact: Displacement means moving something from its usual place or position.
fact: In science, displacement is the amount of water pushed aside when an object is put into water.
fact: «Water displacement» can be used to measure the volume of an odd-shaped object, such as a stone.
fact: «Displacement of people» means forcing people to leave their homes, for example because of war, floods or drought.
fact: In maths and science, displacement can also mean how far, and in what direction, something has moved from its starting point.
exercise: «The strong wind caused a displacement of the tiles on the roof.» What does displacement mean here? | *Moved out of their usual place | Made them cleaner | Lost them in the garden | Turned them into bricks
exercise: «When Mia put the stone in the bucket, the water rose.» In science, what has been displaced? | *The water pushed aside by the stone | The colour of the stone | The bucket itself | The air above the bucket
exercise: «Floods forced the displacement of many families from their homes.» What does displacement of people mean? | A festival of music in the park | Counting people in a park | *Forcing people to leave their homes | Building new homes for everyone
test: «The ball rolled from the start line to the far wall.» What does displacement mean in maths here? | How fast the ball rolled | *How far and in what direction it moved from its start | How heavy the ball is | What colour the ball was
test: Why does a floating ship stay up? | It is lighter than air | *It pushes aside its own weight of water | It has a flat bottom only | It is painted blue
exam: «Using water displacement, Priya found the volume of her odd-shaped stone.» What does water displacement help you do? | Make the water warmer | Stop the stone from floating | Find the weight of the sea | *Find the volume of an odd-shaped object
